*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.exe
/comptime.h
/comptime.bat
/oshw-qt/ui_*.h
/oshw-qt/moc_*.cpp
/build
/build-static
/out
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
build-*/

# Release packaging output (package.ps1): the zip attached to the GitHub
# release, plus the staged folder it is built from. Contains the built exe and
# redistributable DLLs -- artifacts, not source.
/dist/

# Runtime settings. The program writes tw_settings.ini next to itself, and
# running a build from the source tree therefore drops one here. It is personal
# state, not source, and the STOCK copy that ships is generated by package.ps1.
/tw_settings.ini

# The settings staging file (jc-53). savesettings() writes tw_settings.ini.tmp-
# <pid>-<seq> beside the target and moves it into place, so an ordinary run
# drops one here for a moment -- and a run that was killed mid-write leaves one
# for good. Ignoring it is not the cleanup: savesettings() removes it on every
# path out, test/settings_test.c pins that, and test/run-e2e.ps1 asserts none
# survives a real run. This is the belt to those braces, so that a crash during
# a debugging session cannot put one in a commit.
/tw_settings.ini.tmp-*

# Personal state, never source. The program writes solutions into a save directory and settings
# beside itself; both carry the paths and progress of whoever ran it. .gitignore does nothing about
# `git add -f`, which is exactly what happens while reproducing a bug -- so CI checks for these as
# tracked files too. See docs/adr/0005-what-level-data-may-be-committed.md.
*.tws
/save/

# The original Microsoft level set. Copyrighted and not redistributable. sets/cc-ms.dac names it so
# that a player who owns a copy can drop it into data/, but the file itself must never be committed.
CHIPS.dat
CHIPS.DAT
chips.dat

# Test and coverage output.
/test-results/
/coverage-report/
*.gcda
*.gcno
*.gcov

# Written by build.ps1 into the build tree for editors and static analysis; the build tree is
# already ignored, but a hand-run cmake can drop one at the root.
/compile_commands.json

# Auto-generated .dac pairings. createallmissingseries() writes one of these for
# any data/*.dat that has no .dac naming it under the other ruleset -- so merely
# RUNNING the game with -L pointed at this tree drops files into sets/. They are
# runtime artifacts, not source: upstream ships the 15 .dac files it means to.
# (test/run-e2e.ps1 copies sets/ and data/ into a scratch directory so it cannot
# cause this, but a hand-run of the game still can.)
/sets/*.dat-*.dac

# Corpus verification recordings (test/run-corpus.ps1). One directory per build,
# holding a summary.tsv and the verifier's stdout for every set -- hundreds of
# files and megabytes of them. They are evidence for one comparison, not source.
/corpus-*/

# libFuzzer reproducers (test/run-fuzz.sh). A finding is committed to
# test/fuzz/corpus/ deliberately, with a case that replays it; the raw
# artifact directory is scratch and must never be committed.
/test/fuzz/findings/


# Screenshots written by the release playtest script. Looking at one is the
# point; committing it is not -- it is a build artifact of a particular release.
playtest-*.png
